MLE Solution and Efficient Estimator Verification

Previously, for the signal model:

( x(n) = A n^{r} + w(n) )

with:

( w(n) \sim N(0,\sigma^2) )

the derivative of the log-likelihood was obtained as:

( \frac{\partial}{\partial A}
\ln p(X;A)
=
\frac{1}{\sigma^2}
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}
(x(n)-An^{r})n^{r} )

Maximum Likelihood Estimation

Set the derivative equal to zero:

( \sum_{n=0}^{N-1}
(x(n)-An^{r})n^{r} = 0 )

Expanding:

( \sum_{n=0}^{N-1} x(n)n^{r}
-
A\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{2r} = 0 )

Solving for A:

( A
=
\frac{
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} x(n)n^{r}
}{
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{2r}
} )

Using the approximation:

( \sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{2r}
\approx
\frac{N^{2r+1}}{2r+1} )

for large N, the estimator becomes:

( \hat{A}_{ML}
=
\frac{2r+1}{N^{2r+1}}
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{r}x(n) )

Thus, the MLE is a weighted sum of the observations.

Connection with CRLB

Previously, the Fisher Information was:

( I(A)
=
\frac{1}{\sigma^2}
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{2r} )

Hence:

( \frac{1}{I(A)}
=
\frac{\sigma^2}{
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{2r} } )

Now rewrite the score function:

( \frac{\partial}{\partial A}
\ln p(X;A)
=
\frac{1}{\sigma^2}
\left[
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} x(n)n^{r}
-
A\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{2r}
\right] )

Factor the Fisher Information term:

( = I(A)
\left[
\frac{
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} x(n)n^{r}
}{
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{2r}
}
-
A
\right] )

Recognizing:

( g(X)
=
\frac{
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} x(n)n^{r}
}{
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{2r}
} )

we obtain:

( \frac{\partial}{\partial A}
\ln p(X;A)
=
I(A)(g(X)-A) )

This exactly matches the equality condition for the Cramer-Rao Lower Bound.

Therefore:

• The estimator achieves the CRLB.
• The estimator is efficient.
• The estimator is an MVUE.

Final Efficient Estimator

( \hat{A}
=
\frac{
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} x(n)n^{r}
}{
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} n^{2r}
} )

From the normalization constant:

( C=T(X)\frac{e^{(N-1)x_{min}}-1}{N-1} )

the posterior distribution becomes:

( p(\theta\mid X)
=\frac{N-1}{e^{(N-1)x_{min}}-1}
\cdot e^{(N-1)\theta}u(\theta)u(x_{min}-\theta) )

This is a truncated exponential pdf.

The MMSE estimator is:

( \hat{\theta}=E[\theta\mid X] )

Thus:

( \hat{\theta}
=\int_0^{x_{min}} \theta
\frac{N-1}{e^{(N-1)x_{min}}-1}
 e^{(N-1)\theta}d\theta )

Let:

( k=\frac{N-1}{e^{(N-1)x_{min}}-1} )

Then:

( \hat{\theta}
=k\int_0^{x_{min}} \theta e^{(N-1)\theta}d\theta )

Using integration by parts:

( \hat{\theta}
=k\left[
\frac{\theta e^{(N-1)\theta}}{N-1}
-\frac{e^{(N-1)\theta}}{(N-1)^2}
\right]_0^{x_{min}} )

Hence:

( \hat{\theta}
=k\left(
\frac{x_{min}e^{(N-1)x_{min}}}{N-1}
-\frac{1}{(N-1)^2}(e^{(N-1)x_{min}}-1)
\right) )

Therefore:

( \hat{\theta}
=\frac{x_{min}e^{(N-1)x_{min}}}{e^{(N-1)x_{min}}-1}
-\frac{1}{N-1} )
